  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "The **absolute value** of a number is its *distance from zero* when viewed on a number line. \n\nDistances are never negative. Therefore, the absolute value of a number is always positive or zero.\n\nLet's consider the number $-5$ and its position on a number line, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14145654/assets/3072e47752dc69d38f997e926092f60c24966031d0484241fa9c1133d89d1c56.png)\n\n \n\nThe **distance** between $-5$ and zero equals $5$ because $-5$ is five steps away from zero. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14145654/assets/dd155b158a3457eb08a95885461d3ffbd36dd852e1b6c4e9dbda04efed749280.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, *the absolute value of * $-5$ *equals* $5.$ We write this as follows:\n\n$$\n| \\, {-5}  \\, |  = 5\n$$",
